def section(start,end,new):
 global s
 a=s.index(start);b=s.index(end,a);s=s[:a]+new.strip()+'\n\n'+s[b:]
section('## Abstract','Keywords:', '''## Abstract

Microbial life in terrestrial Mars analogue systems must combine cellular maintenance with access to energy and carbon, but shared functional labels need not imply similar genetic repertoires. We compared lineage structure and source-associated variation in selected genomic strategies across seven catalogues and examined their molecular organization in an additional global dryland catalogue. Of 4,213 original metagenome-assembled genomes, 3,904 passed primary quality thresholds. Under source-preserving null models, 42 of 43 variable bacterial maintenance markers and 14 of 15 energy markers were phylogenetically clustered. Among 2,693 bacterial representatives, maintenance and energy portfolios had source partial R² values of 5.01% and 2.19%, respectively. Their paired difference was uncertain under lineage-block Bayesian reweighting (2.81 percentage points; 95% range −1.02 to 5.28) and sensitive to aggregation within the solute and ion maintenance module. Follow-up decomposition statistically localized much of this source structure to Kdp-associated potassium acquisition and regulation. KdpABC marker co-detection retained a descriptive source association within cross-source family support, whereas the additional dryland catalogue lacked sufficient stringent family-level support for an equivalent comparison. Sampling provenance further distinguished regional analogue relevance from the local conditions represented by individual or coassembled genomes. The results identify lineage-structured differences in specified gene combinations, but do not establish universal maintenance convergence, environmental causation or alternative pathways supplied by unknown genes. Resolving adaptation across Mars analogue systems requires linking these molecular units to independently supported environmental contrasts, rather than treating habitat labels or aggregate marker counts as mechanisms.''')
s=s.replace('Here we integrate comparative genomics across seven Mars-analogue catalogues with source-resolved environmental evidence. Completed analyses characterize the lineage structure and source differentiation of selected maintenance and energy functions, assess their sensitivity to annotation and genome quality, and estimate source profiles and within-source associations using Bayesian models. We also organize measured environmental and resource variables at their actual sampling levels and connect existing carbon-fixation route evidence to those units. This retrospective ecological integration establishes what the current data explain and what remains distinguishable only through additional computational comparisons. It provides a basis for relating microbial strategies to particular Martian settings without claiming demonstrated life, activity or resource-production performance on Mars.', '''Here we compare genome-encoded maintenance and resource-acquisition configurations across seven source catalogues, then resolve the sensitivity of a broad maintenance–energy contrast to its constituent molecular systems. We ask whether source differentiation remains distinguishable from lineage structure, whether an aggregate contrast represents several functional groups or a more specific combination, and whether an additional dryland catalogue supplies comparable independent support. A separate provenance audit evaluates the dimensions of Mars analogy and the spatial units represented by those genomes. This design addresses the genomic organization of candidate strategies; it does not assume that a source label measures a particular environmental constraint or that marker occurrence establishes activity.''')
section('### Source differentiation differs between functional categories and domains','### Bayesian source profiles', '''### The maintenance–energy contrast depends on uncertainty and aggregation

Among 2,693 bacterial representatives in 30 eligible cross-source lineage blocks, source partial R² was 5.0064% for maintenance and 2.1932% for energy modules. The maintenance-minus-energy difference was 2.813 percentage points. Paired lineage-block Bayesian reweighting gave a 95% conditional range of −1.016 to 5.280 percentage points; the corresponding stringent-quality analysis of 813 representatives in 24 blocks gave a difference of 4.130 points and a range of 1.297 to 8.071. Multiplying lineage weights by independent source weights yielded ranges crossing zero in both quality strata. These ranges describe sensitivity within the observed catalogues and grouping assumptions, rather than a posterior environmental effect across all Mars analogues (Fig. 2).

Restoring correctly assigned functions gave a primary difference of 2.226 percentage points, again with a lineage-reweighting range crossing zero. Source omissions and individual lineage-block omissions retained the positive point direction, but did not resolve uncertainty about a general category ordering. The primary archaeal comparison had the opposite ordering, with source partial R² values of 8.21% for maintenance and 17.01% for energy. Its 31-representative stringent portfolio subset did not resolve the contrast. The bacterial result therefore does not support a universal difference between all maintenance and energy functions.

The bacterial comparison comprised five maintenance modules and two variable energy modules; its light-associated module was invariant. The solute and ion maintenance module had the largest source partial R², 12.322%. Omitting it reduced the maintenance-minus-energy difference to 0.441 percentage points. In a post-result sensitivity analysis, equally weighting that module's five constituent subgroups reduced the difference to 0.319 points, with a lineage-reweighting range of −2.807 to 2.553. Neither weighting is an established measure of physiological investment. Together these analyses show that the broad category contrast depends on which molecular capabilities are counted and how their coverage is aggregated.

### Source structure is concentrated in potassium-associated combinations

The five solute and ion subgroups had markedly different source associations. In the original primary bacterial analysis, source partial R² was 44.745% for Kdp-associated potassium acquisition and regulation, compared with 0.138–6.381% for the other four subgroups. Equal KO weighting assigned the five subgroups weights proportional to 1:3:3:5:3. Equal subgroup weighting reduced the aggregate module's source partial R² from 12.322% to 2.145%. An exact residual-covariance decomposition showed negative cross terms between several source-associated subgroup components. Such statistical cancellation does not demonstrate competition for cellular resources or replacement of one pathway by another.

To distinguish gene coverage from specified combinations, we separated KdpABC transport markers from KdpDE regulatory markers and evaluated ProVWX, EctABC, EctD and BetAB separately. Within 1,061 representatives from 30 families meeting an exploratory cross-source support criterion, simultaneous detection of the three KdpABC markers retained a descriptive source partial R² of 18.720% after family and quality adjustment. KdpDE co-detection yielded 21.647%, whereas ProVWX, EctABC and BetAB yielded 1.249%, 4.296% and 0.397%, respectively. These associations do not depend solely on counting three Kdp subunits independently (Fig. 3).

KdpABC co-detection nevertheless does not establish a complete KdpFABC operon: KdpF was outside the selected panel, and genomic adjacency was not evaluated here. Kdp participates in potassium homeostasis and is regulated at expression and post-translational levels; its presence is not a salt-tolerance-specific marker or evidence of use [31]. Likewise, partial EctABC or BetAB support does not establish an unknown substitute, because upstream requirements, alternative known routes and detection uncertainty remain unresolved. The follow-up identifies specific molecular candidates for interpretation without assigning the original source contrast to a measured environmental driver.

### The additional dryland catalogue supports combinations but not equivalent ecological replication

The additional catalogue contained 826 quality-screened MAGs, including 783 Bacteria and 43 Archaea. Excluding flagged background genomes and ANI95 clusters shared with the existing 4,095-genome comparison pool, then choosing quality-ranked bacterial representatives independently within each quality stratum, retained 739 primary and 154 stringent representatives. These numbers are not additions to the original 2,693-genome model. Their collection labels covered 25 and 21 sampling groups, respectively, within one additional study.

Among stringent representatives with at least one specified KdpABC marker, 38 of 43 contained all three markers; for ProVWX, 17 of 18 contained all three. Corresponding EctABC and BetAB counts were 1 of 11 and 10 of 39. The marker structure is consistent with multicomponent systems, but does not establish ecological convergence or absence of alternative biosynthetic routes. Conditional site-weighted Bayesian reweighting placed the KdpABC co-detection fraction between 72.8% and 96.8% and ProVWX between 73.7% and 99.9%; these are not tests of equality with the original catalogue.

At the same family-level common-support gate, the primary dryland comparison retained only 147 representatives in five families across 22 sampling groups. The KdpABC co-detection site partial R² was 15.125%, falling to 0.147% after degrees-of-freedom adjustment. No stringent family or order subset met the gate. The adjusted value is not a cross-validated prediction score, and insufficient support does not establish absence of an environmental relationship. It does prevent this catalogue from being presented as an equivalent replication of the original seven-source contrast.

### Mars analogue relevance and sampling support are distinct properties

All 75 deposited dryland field samples were linked to 25 collection labels, but those labels did not represent a uniform spatial scale. The primary representative genomes included 561 assemblies linked to three parent soil samples and 178 linked to a single parent; the stringent subset contained 110 and 44, respectively. Parent samples under Drass, Kalahari, Richtersveld and North Australian Desert labels spanned approximately 54.6, 37.8, 23.6 and 14.0 km. Identical deposited coordinates at other sites describe metadata precision rather than proof that the samples occupied the same microhabitat. Coassembly parentage does not provide abundance or locate a recovered genotype in each parent sample.

We therefore treated regional analogue evidence, sample substrate and sampling geometry as separate attributes. Six regional candidates prioritized for a bounded literature audit—Atacama, McMurdo Dry Valleys, Mount Seuss, Mojave, Namib and Negev—contained 301 primary and 70 stringent representatives. These counts describe an audit subset, not a final six-site definition of Mars analogues. Regional evidence cannot certify a deposited Nevada Mojave soil as the carbonate bedrock examined in California, or a Yungay surface soil as the wet clay horizon reported at approximately 30 cm depth [33, 34, 35]. High-elevation Dry Valley ground-ice studies likewise do not establish the ice state at our deposited McMurdo point [36]. The Mount Seuss comparison has same-region soil ecological evidence [37], whereas the bounded Namib and Negev sources address aeolian cementation and radar detection, respectively [38, 39]. These sources establish different dimensions of analogy rather than interchangeable ecological environments. The remaining 19 labels were retained as broader dryland context with unresolved analogue-specific scope, rather than declared non-analogues.

Even in the six-candidate subset, the cross-group support criterion retained only 41 primary representatives in two families; no stringent class, order or family subset met it. These results delimit an available design, not a biological absence. Analogue eligibility and statistical comparability must both be established before asking which environmental constraint explains a combination. Regional analogue literature and its correspondence limits are recorded separately (Supplementary Table S1).''')
