section('### From shared functions to conditional strategy combinations','### Explaining a source effect', '''### Molecular organization changes the interpretation of broad strategy labels

Shared maintenance labels concealed unequal source structure among the selected molecular capabilities. Strong phylogenetic clustering was accompanied by source-associated differences, but the paired maintenance–energy contrast remained uncertain under source composition sensitivity and changed substantially when the solute and ion module was reweighted. The most specific supported observation is therefore heterogeneity among gene combinations, including Kdp-associated transport and regulation, rather than a general failure of maintenance convergence. Gene coverage and co-detection answer different questions and neither alone measures physiological investment.

This distinction sharpens the ecological question. Potassium acquisition, compatible-solute transport and solute biosynthesis can contribute to cellular maintenance through different physiological requirements. A shared label should not be assumed to make their responses to a resource or constraint interchangeable. Kdp co-detection offers a concrete candidate for subsequent sequence-context and environmental tests, but its post-result identification and the absence of direct potassium-availability contrasts preclude calling it the mechanism underlying the catalogue pattern. Total potassium, if available, would also require distinction from bioavailable potassium.

Recent desert and brine studies provide complementary resource contexts [7, 8]. Their observations motivate asking when organisms encode inorganic energy acquisition, carbon assimilation and heterotrophic capabilities together. They do not establish that different study-level patterns result from the same environmental axis. Our comparison preserves these separate functions and avoids reducing them to mutually exclusive autotroph and heterotroph strategies. A mechanistic account must explain the particular combination within its lineage and sampling context.''')
s=s.replace('A decisive extension would compare survival and acquisition traits against separately measured constraints and resource descriptors, accounting for their support ranges and sampling hierarchy. Public soil MAGs are potentially valuable when they add a missing contrast, such as comparable water limitation across different carbon contexts.', 'A decisive extension would compare the specified molecular combinations against separately measured constraints and resource descriptors, accounting for their support ranges and sampling hierarchy. The dryland extension demonstrates why a large public MAG collection is not automatically a crossed design: many genomes derive from coassemblies, regional labels differ in spatial extent, and stringent common-lineage support is sparse. Public soil MAGs are valuable when they add a missing contrast, such as comparable water limitation across different carbon contexts.')
s=s.replace('The central unresolved issue is which combinations of constraints and resources predict strategy differences beyond lineage and source background. Our integrated evidence makes this question concrete: genomic configurations vary, environmental support is heterogeneous, and a sparse carbon-fixation endpoint cannot alone carry the proposed interaction. Addressing the question requires targeted public-data contrasts and reaction definitions that connect the observed variation to mechanisms. It does not require abandoning the Mars-analogue focus or presenting unmeasured fluxes as established adaptations.', 'The central unresolved issue is which environmental and resource contrasts predict differences in specific gene combinations beyond lineage and sampling background. The present results locate a molecular source of aggregate heterogeneity and establish the limits of its independent support. They provide a genome-resolved starting point for studying adaptation across Mars analogue systems, while leaving environmental causation and realized resource use open to tests with matched public data.')
s=s.replace('This manuscript reuses completed analyses and adds a measurement-resolved evidence inventory and linkage of existing route calls.', 'This study combines the original comparative-genomic analyses with paired uncertainty estimation, post-result module decomposition, specified gene-combination diagnostics and an additional dryland provenance audit.')
s=s.replace('The contrast was R²energy − R²maintenance.', 'For the paired analyses reported here, the contrast is R²maintenance − R²energy; historical outputs using the reverse sign were converted explicitly.')
insert='''### Paired uncertainty and molecular decomposition

For each of 2,000 reweighting replicates, independent Exp(1) weights were assigned to observed lineage blocks, shared by all genomes in a block and all functional endpoints. Reduced and full weighted models were refitted, with response scaling recalculated under those weights. Normalized weights correspond to a Bayesian bootstrap on the observed blocks [32]. A separate sensitivity multiplied these weights by independent source-level Exp(1) weights, examining dependence on the observed source composition rather than guaranteeing population-level coverage for the nonlinear R² contrast. The random seed was 20261001. The blocks are not randomly sampled independent evolutionary or environmental units; all intervals are conditional on this approximation and the observed catalogues.

Post-result diagnostics omitted individual modules, KOs and lineage blocks, retained all module reassignment comparisons and compared KO weighting with equal weighting of the five existing solute and ion subgroups. Equal subgroup weighting was not substituted as a new primary endpoint. For residual covariance matrices C_reduced and C_full and subgroup weight vector w, source partial R² was wᵀ(C_reduced − C_full)w / (wᵀC_reduced w). Diagonal and cross terms were retained. Different weights define different responses, and cross terms were not interpreted as physiological interactions.

Specified combinations were KdpABC (K01546, K01547, K01548), KdpDE (K07646, K07667), ProVWX (K02000, K02001, K02002), EctABC (K06718, K00836, K06720), EctD (K10674) and BetAB (K00108, K00130). Calls distinguished none, some and all specified markers detected in a MAG; "all" did not mean a complete pathway or active system. Definitions requiring restored markers used the existing restoration presence calls. The exploratory common-support criterion required a lineage with at least two sources or sampling groups each containing five representatives, then retained all representatives in each eligible lineage. Quality and rank indicators formed the reduced model, and source or sampling-group indicators the full model. Descriptive degrees-of-freedom adjustment used 1 − [SSE_full/(n−rank_full)]/[SSE_reduced/(n−rank_reduced)]. It was not a holdout score or hypothesis test. Low state counts were flagged rather than used to select favourable endpoints.

### Additional dryland provenance and analogue scope

The additional catalogue was linked to PRJNA832417 field samples through deposited assembly parent records. Existing CheckM2, GTDB and full KOfam outputs were reused; no functional endpoints from digitized community plots entered this analysis. Bacterial representatives were selected after quality filtering, background flags and removal of ANI95 groups shared with the existing 4,095-genome comparison pool. Ranking used completeness minus five times contamination, followed by N50 and identifier. Primary and stringent selection were performed separately. The 13 primary and five stringent selected ANI groups occurring at multiple sampling labels retained their full label sets; exclusion of these groups provided a sensitivity analysis.

Reported parent latitude and longitude were parsed without correcting deposited positions. Great-circle distance used the haversine formula with radius 6371.0088 km; the maximum pairwise distance described each parent-sample group. Shared coordinates were not treated as evidence of exact co-location. The six regional candidates were prioritized in the regional scope review rather than selected by gene-state differences, but the audit followed inspection of catalogue outcomes and is explicitly retrospective. A bounded primary-literature audit recorded regional support, the studied locality and substrate, the relevant Mars analogy and correspondence limits. Analogue relevance was not converted to a severity score, and unresolved regional coverage was not treated as evidence of non-analogy. Common-lineage support was evaluated on metadata without loading functional responses. Existing site chemistry, transferred regional values and unmeasured quantities retained distinct evidence status; no missing resource values were imputed in this analysis.

'''
s=s.replace('### Bayesian functional-support models',insert+'### Bayesian functional-support models')
